  "problem": "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3 & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2}-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
